    %%rbac role:radiologist on ImagingReport.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on ImagingReport.delete

    %%field Medication.code help: The catalogue's own code for this medicine. Quoted on a prescription and on a stock order.
    %%field Medication.name help: The generic name, which is what a prescription is written in.
    %%field Medication.brand_name help: The brand it is stocked as, where that differs. Shown to the person picking it off the shelf.
    %%field Medication.form help: What it physically is — a tablet, an injection, a cream. Decides how it is given and how it is counted.
    %%field Medication.strength help: The strength of one unit, as printed on the packaging, such as 500 mg.
    %%field Medication.route help: The route it is normally given by. A prescription item may narrow this but not contradict it.
    %%field Medication.therapeutic_class help: The class of medicine this belongs to. This is what the duplication rule reads: a second medicine of the same class is the clash a hospital without a full formulary can actually detect.
    %%field Medication.is_controlled help: Whether it is a controlled drug. Controlled drugs cannot be dispensed by the person who prescribed them.
    %%field Medication.is_high_alert help: Marks a medicine where an error causes serious harm — insulin, heparin, an opioid. A high-alert item needs a second qualified check before it goes on a prescription.
    %%field Medication.is_active help: Whether it may still be prescribed. A withdrawn medicine is deactivated rather than deleted, because the prescriptions that named it must still resolve.
    %%field Medication.inventory_item_id help: The stocked item this medicine is held as. Empty for a medicine the hospital does not stock — a patient's own supply, or one ordered in for them. It is what lets dispensing decrement the store, which without this link it could not find.
    %%field Medication.contraindication_notes help: What the prescriber should know before choosing it, in the pharmacy's own words. Not a substitute for a formulary — see the model's scope.
    %%field Medication.form enum: MedicationForm
    %%field Medication.route enum: MedicationRoute
    %%index Medication(therapeutic_class)
    %%index Medication(is_controlled, is_active)
    %%rbac role:doctor|nurse|pharmacist|hospital_manager|administrator on Medication.read
    %%rbac role:pharmacist|administrator on Medication.create
    %%rbac role:pharmacist|administrator on Medication.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on Medication.delete

    %%field Prescription.prescription_number help: The pharmacy's own number for this prescription. Generated on saving, never typed.
    %%field Prescription.encounter_id help: The clinical contact it was written during.
    %%field Prescription.prescribed_by_id help: Who wrote it. Held as a member of staff rather than as a doctor so that it can be compared with who dispensed it — that comparison is the controlled-drug rule.
    %%field Prescription.status help: Where it has got to, from written to collected. Driven by the lifecycle.
    %%field Prescription.has_controlled_item help: Whether any item on this prescription is a controlled drug. Written by the application when an item is added, never by hand, and it is what the segregation rule reads — a rule can only see the row it is given, so the fact has to be on it.
    %%field Prescription.is_discharge_prescription help: Marks a prescription the patient takes home rather than one given on the ward. It is dispensed differently and it goes on the discharge summary.
    %%field Prescription.prescribed_at help: When it was written.
    %%field Prescription.verified_by_id help: The pharmacist who checked it. Empty until one has.
    %%field Prescription.verified_at help: When it was checked.
    %%field Prescription.dispensed_by_id help: Who handed out the medicine. For a controlled drug this may not be the person who prescribed it.
    %%field Prescription.dispensed_at help: When it was dispensed.
    %%field Prescription.notes help: Anything the prescriber wants pharmacy to know.
    %%field Prescription.status enum: PrescriptionStatus
    %%index Prescription(status, prescribed_at)
    %%index Prescription(encounter_id, prescribed_at)
    %%rbac role:doctor|nurse|pharmacist|administrator on Prescription.read
    %%rbac role:doctor on Prescription.create
    %%rbac role:doctor|pharmacist|nurse on Prescription.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on Prescription.delete

    %%field PrescriptionItem.prescription_id help: The prescription this line belongs to.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.medication_id help: Which medicine. Chosen from the catalogue rather than typed, so that the controlled and high-alert flags are the catalogue's and not the prescriber's memory.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.dose help: How much is given at once, such as 500 mg or 10 mL.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.route help: How it is given for this patient. Narrows the medicine's usual route.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.frequency help: How often, in the words the prescription is written in — three times a day, at night, every four hours.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.quantity help: How much to dispense in total. This is what pharmacy counts out and what stock is decremented by.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.is_prn help: Marks a medicine given as required rather than on a schedule. A nurse decides when; the prescription decides the limit.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.is_high_alert help: The medicine's high-alert flag as it stood when this was prescribed. Copied from the catalogue by the application, for the same reason the invoice line keeps its own price: the rule that demands a second check reads this row and nothing else.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.duration_days help: How many days the course runs for. Empty for a medicine taken indefinitely.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.second_check_by_id help: The second qualified person who checked a high-alert medicine. Required for one, and empty for everything else.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.instructions help: How to take it, in the words that go on the label — with food, before bed.
    %%field PrescriptionItem.route enum: MedicationRoute
    %%index PrescriptionItem(prescription_id)
    %%index PrescriptionItem(medication_id)
    %%rbac role:doctor|nurse|pharmacist|administrator on PrescriptionItem.read
    %%rbac role:doctor on PrescriptionItem.create
    %%rbac role:doctor|pharmacist on PrescriptionItem.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on PrescriptionItem.delete

    %%field ChargeableItem.code help: The price list's own code for this charge, quoted on an invoice line and in a price review.
    %%field ChargeableItem.name help: What the charge is called on the bill the patient reads.
    %%field ChargeableItem.category help: What kind of charge it is. Decides which part of the episode it is grouped under on the invoice.
    %%field ChargeableItem.unit_price help: What one of them costs today. An invoice line keeps its own copy, so changing this never rewrites a bill already raised.
    %%field ChargeableItem.is_active help: Whether it may still be charged. A withdrawn charge is deactivated rather than deleted, because the invoice lines that used it must still resolve.
    %%field ChargeableItem.description help: What the charge covers, for the person choosing it and for the patient querying it.
    %%field ChargeableItem.category enum: ChargeCategory
    %%index ChargeableItem(category, is_active)
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on ChargeableItem.read
    %%rbac role:hospital_manager|administrator on ChargeableItem.create
    %%rbac role:hospital_manager|administrator on ChargeableItem.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on ChargeableItem.delete

    %%field Invoice.invoice_number help: The hospital's own number for this bill. Generated on saving, never typed, and quoted on every payment and claim.
    %%field Invoice.patient_id help: Whose episode this is. The one anchor every invoice has, whether it prices an outpatient contact or a whole stay.
    %%field Invoice.status help: Where the bill has got to, from drafted to settled. Driven by the lifecycle.
    %%field Invoice.gross_amount help: What the episode cost in total, before the policy is applied. The sum of the lines.
    %%field Invoice.insurer_amount help: The share the insurer is being asked for.
    %%field Invoice.patient_amount help: The share the patient owes themselves — the co-payment. Computed from the policy, not typed.
    %%field Invoice.paid_amount help: How much has actually been received against this bill so far, from either payer.
    %%field Invoice.policy_verified help: Whether the cover was checked against the dates being charged and passed. Set by the application when the co-payment is applied; a bill cannot be issued while it is false.
    %%field Invoice.issued_on help: The day the bill was raised. Empty while it is still a draft.
    %%field Invoice.due_on help: The day it falls due. Nothing may be marked overdue before this date has passed.
    %%field Invoice.encounter_id help: The outpatient contact being billed, where the episode is one contact.
    %%field Invoice.admission_id help: The inpatient stay being billed, where the episode is a whole admission. An invoice carries one or the other, never neither.
    %%field Invoice.insurance_policy_id help: The cover being claimed against. Empty for a self-paying patient, and the whole bill is then the patient's.
    %%field Invoice.notes help: Anything the billing office needs to record about this bill.
    %%field Invoice.status enum: InvoiceStatus
    %%index Invoice(status, due_on)
    %%index Invoice(patient_id, issued_on)
    %%index Invoice(insurance_policy_id, status)
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on Invoice.read
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on Invoice.create
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on Invoice.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on Invoice.delete

    %%field InvoiceLine.invoice_id help: The bill this charge is on.
    %%field InvoiceLine.chargeable_item_id help: Which charge from the price list. Chosen rather than typed, so two bills for the same procedure agree.
    %%field InvoiceLine.description help: What the line says on the bill. Copied from the price list when the line is raised, so a later rename does not rewrite an old invoice.
    %%field InvoiceLine.quantity help: How many — nights, tests, tablets, hours.
    %%field InvoiceLine.unit_price help: What one cost on the day it was charged. Held here rather than looked up, so the bill reprints identically years later.
    %%field InvoiceLine.line_amount help: Quantity times unit price. Refused if the three disagree.
    %%field InvoiceLine.charged_on help: The day the thing charged for actually happened, which is not always the day the bill was raised.
    %%field InvoiceLine.source_reference help: What in the record this charge came from — a procedure code, a lab order number, a range of bed-nights. Free text: the model has no one entity a charge points back at, and four nullable references would be worse than a legible sentence.
    %%index InvoiceLine(invoice_id)
    %%index InvoiceLine(chargeable_item_id, charged_on)
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on InvoiceLine.read
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on InvoiceLine.create
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on InvoiceLine.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on InvoiceLine.delete

    %%field Payment.receipt_number help: The receipt this payment was given. Generated on saving, never typed.
    %%field Payment.invoice_id help: The bill the money was received against.
    %%field Payment.received_by_id help: The member of staff who took the payment. Every receipt has a name against it.
    %%field Payment.payer_type help: Who paid — the insurer settling a claim, the patient paying their share, or a third party.
    %%field Payment.method help: How the money arrived. Decides what reconciling it against the bank looks like.
    %%field Payment.amount help: How much was received. Refused if it is not positive, or if it is more than the bill still owes.
    %%field Payment.outstanding_before help: What the bill still owed immediately before this payment. Resolved by the application when the receipt is written, and it is what makes both the overpayment refusal and the settlement decision answerable from the receipt alone.
    %%field Payment.received_on help: The day the money was received, which is not always the day it was recorded.
    %%field Payment.bank_reference help: The reference the money arrived with, for reconciling it against a bank statement.
    %%field Payment.payer_type enum: PayerType
    %%field Payment.method enum: PaymentMethod
    %%index Payment(invoice_id, received_on)
    %%index Payment(payer_type, received_on)
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on Payment.read
    %%rbac role:billing_clerk|hospital_manager|administrator on Payment.create
    %%rbac role:hospital_manager|administrator on Payment.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on Payment.delete

    %%field Supplier.code help: The purchasing department's own code for this supplier.
    %%field Supplier.name help: The company as it appears on an order and an invoice.
    %%field Supplier.lead_time_days help: How many days a delivery takes once ordered. A reorder level means nothing without it — the level exists to cover exactly this many days of use.
    %%field Supplier.is_active help: Whether they may still be ordered from. Deactivated rather than deleted, because the deliveries they made must still resolve.
    %%field Supplier.contact_name help: Who to speak to about an order.
    %%field Supplier.email help: Where an order is sent.
    %%field Supplier.phone help: The number to chase a late delivery on.
    %%field Supplier.address_line help: Where they are, for returns and correspondence.
    %%field Supplier.notes help: Anything purchasing needs to remember about them — minimum order, delivery days, account number.
    %%index Supplier(is_active)
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|pharmacist|hospital_manager|administrator on Supplier.read
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on Supplier.create
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on Supplier.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on Supplier.delete

    %%field InventoryItem.code help: The store's own code for this item, on the shelf label and every requisition.
    %%field InventoryItem.name help: What it is called by the people who order and use it.
    %%field InventoryItem.supplier_id help: Who it is bought from, and who a replenishment is raised against.
    %%field InventoryItem.category help: What kind of stock it is. Decides where it is stored and who counts it.
    %%field InventoryItem.unit_of_issue help: What one unit is, in the words the store uses — each, box of 100, 500 mL bottle. Every quantity in the system is in these units.
    %%field InventoryItem.quantity_on_hand help: How many are in the store now. This is the sum of every movement against the item and is never typed in directly.
    %%field InventoryItem.reorder_level help: The quantity at which more must be ordered. Set to cover the supplier's lead time at the expected rate of use.
    %%field InventoryItem.reorder_quantity help: How many to order when it falls to that level.
    %%field InventoryItem.requires_cold_chain help: Marks stock that must be kept refrigerated. It changes where it is stored, how it is delivered, and what happens if a fridge fails.
    %%field InventoryItem.is_on_reorder help: Set by the replenishment workflow when the item falls to its reorder level, and cleared when the delivery is received. It is what puts the item on the buyer's worklist.
    %%field InventoryItem.is_active help: Whether it is still stocked. A discontinued item is deactivated rather than deleted, because the movements against it must still resolve.
    %%field InventoryItem.storage_location help: Where in the store it lives — the aisle, shelf or fridge.
    %%field InventoryItem.category enum: StockCategory
    %%index InventoryItem(is_on_reorder, is_active)
    %%index InventoryItem(category, storage_location)
    %%index InventoryItem(supplier_id)
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|pharmacist|nurse|ward_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on InventoryItem.read
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on InventoryItem.create
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on InventoryItem.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on InventoryItem.delete

    %%field StockTransaction.inventory_item_id help: Which item moved.
    %%field StockTransaction.performed_by_id help: Who moved it. Every movement has a name against it, because an unexplained count difference is investigated by asking that person.
    %%field StockTransaction.transaction_type help: What kind of movement this is. It decides which direction the quantity must go, and a movement whose sign contradicts its type is refused.
    %%field StockTransaction.quantity help: How many units moved, in the item's unit of issue. Positive adds to the store, negative takes away.
    %%field StockTransaction.quantity_on_hand_after help: The running balance after this movement — a stock ledger's own column, and what makes a count difference traceable to the movement that caused it.
    %%field StockTransaction.triggers_reorder help: Set by the application when this movement takes the item to or below its reorder level and it is not already on the buyer's worklist. It is what the replenishment workflow fires on.
    %%field StockTransaction.occurred_on help: When the movement actually happened, which is not always when it was entered.
    %%field StockTransaction.supplier_id help: Who delivered it. Set on a receipt and empty on everything else.
    %%field StockTransaction.ward_id help: Where an issue went. Set on an issue and empty on everything else, and it is how consumption is attributed to a ward.
    %%field StockTransaction.batch_number help: The manufacturer's batch, which is what a recall is traced by.
    %%field StockTransaction.expiry_date help: When this batch expires. Empty for stock that does not expire.
    %%field StockTransaction.reference help: The delivery note, requisition or stock-count reference this movement came from.
    %%field StockTransaction.notes help: Why, where the type alone does not say — a correction after a count, a disposal after a fridge failure.
    %%field StockTransaction.transaction_type enum: StockTransactionType
    %%index StockTransaction(inventory_item_id, occurred_on)
    %%index StockTransaction(transaction_type, occurred_on)
    %%index StockTransaction(batch_number)
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|pharmacist|nurse|ward_manager|hospital_manager|administrator on StockTransaction.read
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|pharmacist|nurse|administrator on StockTransaction.create
    %%rbac role:inventory_manager|administrator on StockTransaction.update
    %%rbac role:administrator on StockTransaction.delete

    %%entity Department help: An organisational unit of the hospital, such as Cardiology or Radiology. It employs staff, runs wards, and is what an appointment or an encounter is booked against.
    %%entity Ward help: A physical place holding beds, run by one department. Distinct from the department: a department may run several wards.
    %%entity Bed help: One trackable bed in a ward, with its own type and occupancy. Managed as a pool — an admission takes one out of it and discharge gives it back.
    %%entity Staff help: Anyone who works here and signs in. Deactivated when they leave rather than deleted, because the notes, orders and results they signed must still resolve years later.
    %%entity Doctor help: The clinical extension of a staff record for someone who may lead an encounter, perform a procedure and prescribe. Carries the specialty and the registration number a nurse record does not.
    %%entity Nurse help: The clinical extension of a staff record for nursing staff. Carries the grade and the home ward, which decide what a nurse may do and where they normally do it.
    %%entity Patient help: The master patient record, created at first contact and kept for decades. Everything clinical, and everything billable, hangs off one.
    %%entity InsurancePolicy help: The cover a patient carries: the insurer, the policy number, the dates it is valid between, and the share of a bill the patient pays themselves.
    %%entity Appointment help: A booked future slot with a doctor. Created by reception; becomes an encounter when the patient actually arrives.
    %%entity Encounter help: One clinical contact between a patient and a clinician — the spine of the record. Notes, observations, diagnoses, procedures and every order hang off it, and closing it is what makes it billable.
    %%entity Admission help: One inpatient stay, from taking a bed to giving it back. A transfer between wards moves the bed on this record rather than starting a new one, so one stay is always one admission.
    %%entity ClinicalNote help: One written note recorded during an encounter, by the member of staff who wrote it. Read as part of the encounter it belongs to, never on its own.
    %%entity VitalSign help: One set of observations — temperature, pulse, blood pressure, oxygen saturation — taken at one moment during an encounter.
    %%entity Diagnosis help: A conclusion recorded against an encounter, with its coding and whether it is the primary reason for the contact.
    %%entity Procedure help: Something done to the patient during an encounter. It is what a consent record attaches to, and what the theatre and billing sides both refer back to.
    %%entity Consent help: A patient's recorded consent to one procedure: who consented, when, and who witnessed it. Kept as a record rather than a flag so that "was this consented?" is a question the application can refuse a write on.
    %%entity LabOrder help: A request for a laboratory test, raised during an encounter and owned by the member of staff responsible for it.
    %%entity LabResult help: One measured value against a lab order, with the reference range it is read against and the person who verified it. An unverified result is not clinically actionable.
    %%entity ImagingOrder help: A request for an imaging study, raised during an encounter.
    %%entity ImagingReport help: The radiologist's written interpretation of an imaging study. Prose, not a value — which is why it is a different thing from a lab result and not a variation of one.
    %%entity Medication help: The drug catalogue: what may be prescribed, in what form and strength, and whether it is a controlled drug. Referenced by prescription items rather than typed into them.
    %%entity Prescription help: What was prescribed at one encounter, by one prescriber, for one patient. Verified and dispensed by pharmacy before the patient collects it.
    %%entity PrescriptionItem help: One drug on a prescription, with its dose, route and duration. Meaningless away from the prescription it belongs to.
    %%entity ChargeableItem help: The price list: what a consultation, a bed-night, a test, an image or a drug costs. Invoice lines are priced from this rather than typed, so two invoices for the same procedure agree.
    %%entity Invoice help: One episode of care, priced. Created when an encounter or an admission closes, settled against the insurance policy and the patient's own share.
    %%entity InvoiceLine help: One charge on an invoice, priced from a chargeable item. Held on the line rather than recomputed, so an invoice reprints identically years later.
    %%entity Payment help: Money received against an invoice, from the insurer or from the patient, in whole or in part.
    %%entity Supplier help: Who consumables and drugs are bought from, and who a replenishment is raised against.
    %%entity InventoryItem help: A stocked consumable with a quantity on hand and a reorder level. Falling to that level is what raises a replenishment.
    %%entity StockTransaction help: One movement that changes a quantity — a receipt, an issue to a ward, a correction after a count, or a return. The quantity on hand is the sum of these and is never typed in directly.

    %%entity ClinicalNote parent: Encounter
    %%entity VitalSign parent: Encounter
    %%entity Diagnosis parent: Encounter
    %%entity Procedure parent: Encounter
    %%entity LabResult parent: LabOrder
    %%entity ImagingReport parent: ImagingOrder
    %%entity PrescriptionItem parent: Prescription
    %%entity InvoiceLine parent: Invoice

